No—not as the proposed two-arm comparison.

The actual object is a usable project name, and the acting subject is the human Principal. The history shows that both arms passed through a novelty-oriented archive and a later suitability selector. That production process—not retrieval alone—may explain their shared concentration around repair, fire, crack, workshop, and inheritance. The organizing contradiction is that the experiment rewards divergence, while the Principal needs a concise, adoptable name.

Next action: create a blinded three-arm packet:

- selected memory-run candidates;
- selected retrieval-run candidates;
- direct naming candidates generated from the same initial brief, without archive mutation.

Normalize presentation and length, conceal provenance, and ask the Principal first to mark names they would actually use, then rank acceptable choices. Include “none.”

Evidence limits: token cost and source participation are established; aesthetic superiority, practical usability, and retrieval’s causal contribution are not. The current selector also confounds the comparison.

Decision boundary: send only when the packet can test adoption against a direct baseline under matched presentation. Choose retrieval only if it produces a materially higher rate of acceptable or selected names that justifies its added cost. If the immediate goal is merely to gather preferences—not compare methods—the current packet may be sent, but it must not support claims that retrieval improved naming quality.